.TH TERMUX-MUSCLE 1 "2026-10-09" "Termux Muscle 0.20.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
termux-muscle \- install, run, diagnose and maintain Claude Code on Android
Termux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B termux-muscle
.RB [ \-\-root " DIR" ]
.RB [ \-\-prefix " TERMUX_PREFIX" ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
.B termux-muscle run
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ CLAUDE_ARGUMENTS ...]
.PP
.B claude
.RI [ CLAUDE_ARGUMENTS ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Termux Muscle manages Claude Code in native Android Termux on ARM64.
It installs the official Linux ARM64 musl executable with an Alpine musl
loader and runs it as an ordinary Termux process, or, on request, inside a
small PRoot namespace; see BACKENDS.
Its management tool is Bash plus a C helper compiled on the device.
It does not compile Claude Code.
.PP
Runtime installation, update and repair verify source archives and selected
files before testing a candidate's version and help output.
A release other than the one this project version was tested with must also
carry Anthropic's signature; see VERSION SELECTION.
Only an accepted candidate becomes current.
The previous release remains available for rollback, and running sessions
hold leases that protect their files from cleanup.
.PP
An ordinary run preserves the caller's arguments, working directory and
Claude account configuration.
Authentication, account entitlements and model safeguards remain controlled
by the upstream service.
A successful local startup does not prove that a particular model, tool,
background workflow or Android configuration works.
.PP
Termux Muscle is an independent Octocore Autonomous Systems community project.
It is not affiliated with or authorized by Anthropic.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-root " DIR"
Select a separate managed installation.
The default is
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/termux-muscle
when XDG_DATA_HOME is set, or
.I $HOME/.local/share/termux-muscle
otherwise.
An arbitrary directory is never treated as an owned installation to delete.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " DIR"
Select the native Termux package prefix containing Bash, patchelf, PRoot,
ripgrep, certificates and other dependencies.
The default is PREFIX, or
.I /data/data/com.termux/files/usr
when PREFIX is unset.
This option does not provide another architecture or Linux distribution.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the Termux Muscle project version on the first line.
When an installation has an active release, a second line names its
Claude Code version, for example
.BR "Claude Code 2.1.283 (active)" .
The first line never changes format, and a missing or damaged installation
only omits the second line.
To ask the Claude Code executable itself, use
.BR "termux-muscle run -- --version" .
.TP
.BR help " [" COMMAND "], " \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the command overview.
It groups commands by what they act on: Claude Code, Termux Muscle itself,
and troubleshooting checks that span the device, the manager and Claude Code.
.B help
.I COMMAND
prints the same help as
.BR "termux-muscle COMMAND --help" .
.PP
Put global path options before the command for clarity.
Arguments after
.B run
belong to Claude Code; place manager path options before
.BR run .
.SH COMMANDS
.PP
Public command help is available with
.BR "termux-muscle COMMAND --help" .
The Bash completion script is installed at
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/bash-completion/completions/termux-muscle
when XDG_DATA_HOME is set, otherwise at
.IR $HOME/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/termux-muscle .
Load the Bash completion package or source that file directly.
An unrelated existing completion file is preserved, and uninstall removes
only the managed symlink.
Completion stops at
.BR run ,
whose following arguments belong to Claude Code.
.TP
.BR install " [" OPTIONS "]"
Install Claude Code: by default the release that Anthropic's latest channel
names.
If an owned installation already exists, prepare and validate a candidate
before selecting it.
The project pin and its source digests come from the installed project's
.I compatibility.json
file; select it with
.BR "\-\-claude-version pinned" .
After activation, installation normally manages the Claude convenience entries,
backing up an existing command before replacing it.
.B \-\-no-link
preserves those command entries for an isolated or custom installation.
The legacy
.B \-\-link
option explicitly requests the same default linking behavior.
See VERSION SELECTION for the shared install/update options.
.TP
.BR run " [\-\-] [" CLAUDE_ARGUMENTS "...]"
Start the current Claude Code release with ordinary authentication and settings.
No model request is implied by the manager itself; the forwarded Claude
arguments and interactive actions determine what Claude does.
For example,
.B termux-muscle run -- auth login
starts the upstream authentication flow.
.PP
.RS
The managed
.B claude
entry is a convenience launcher for this command.
Leading upstream
.B update
or
.B upgrade
commands are redirected to the manager's runtime update operation.
A leading upstream
.B install
command prints a managed-installation notice.
Claude's unmanaged background updater is disabled for this backend.
.RE
.TP
.BR update " [" OPTIONS "]"
Prepare, verify and probe a runtime candidate before changing the active
release.
Without a version selector, follow Anthropic's latest release channel.
When the active release is already the one the channel names, report that and
change nothing.
A channel never replaces the active release with an older one; name an older
release exactly to install it.
A failed download, signature check or candidate probe leaves the current
release selected.
The managed
.B claude update
runs this command.
Update Termux Muscle itself with
.BR self-update .
.TP
.B rollback
Probe and restore the previous validated local runtime, without downloading it.
Fail if no previous release is available or it no longer passes validation.
This restores the Claude Code runtime, not an older Termux Muscle project
release.
.TP
.BR repair " [\-\-offline]"
Rebuild the current runtime from its recorded original sources and digests,
then validate and activate the replacement.
Preserve its exact version and pinned, signed or unverified provenance.
Repair does not silently select latest, change credentials or repair account
access.
Offline repair requires the necessary verified cached archives.
.TP
.BR versions " [\-\-json]"
Print current, previous and retained Claude Code versions with their source
policy.
.B \-\-json
prints the managed state record, including release identifiers.
This is distinct from the allowlisted public diagnostic report and can contain
local lifecycle metadata.
.TP
.BR "versions \-\-available" " [\-\-all] [\-\-json]"
List Claude Code releases published for the official ARM64 musl package,
newest first, with their release dates.
Each row is labelled
.B pinned
(the version this Termux Muscle release was accepted with),
.B formerly pinned
(a version an earlier Termux Muscle release was accepted with; the label names
the first and last such release, from the manifest's pin history) or
.BR unverified
(never accepted by this project), and also
.B active
or
.B retained
when it is installed, plus upstream tags such as
.BR latest .
The ten newest releases are shown, together with every pinned, formerly
pinned, active or retained one;
.B \-\-all
lists every release.
.B \-\-json
prints a
.B termux-muscle.available.v1
object; each release carries a
.B formerly_pinned
field with its history entry or null, and the object carries the manifest's
.B pin_history
array.
The listing reads one document from the official npm registry over HTTPS
without following redirects; it downloads no archive and changes no state.
The labels describe this project's device acceptance.
Installing a listed release still requires Anthropic's signature on it,
whatever its label.
.TP
.BR doctor " [\-\-output FILE]"
Check local resolver configuration, managed link ownership, runtime integrity,
version/help startup and the shell environment the release's backend gives
Claude Code.
No model request, installation, update, link repair or upload is performed.
Print JSON to standard output by default.
See DIAGNOSTIC REPORTS for output-file behavior and limitations.
.TP
.BR test " [\-\-output FILE] [\-\-model MODEL_ID ...]"
Collect a sanitized device and installation report using the same safe local
checks by default.
Each explicit
.BI \-\-model " MODEL_ID"
permits an authenticated probe of that exact model and may consume account
usage.
Repeat the option to request several model checks.
The report distinguishes requested and actually observed model IDs;
fallback is not reported as success for the requested model.
.B doctor
never accepts model checks.
.TP
.BR link " [\-\-replace] [\-\-path PATH]"
Manage a Claude convenience entry, normally
.IR $PREFIX/bin/claude .
A missing entry can be created and an unchanged owned entry reused.
An unrelated existing entry is preserved unless
.B \-\-replace
is explicitly supplied.
Replacement preserves the original regular-file bytes and permissions, or
the original literal symlink target, for eligible restoration on uninstall.
.BI \-\-path " PATH"
selects another entry; its parent directory must already exist.
.PP
.RS
If another installer or the user later changes the managed entry,
uninstall preserves that replacement and retains restoration evidence.
Use
.B doctor
to detect drift and
.B termux-muscle run
to run the managed runtime directly while investigating.
.RE
.TP
.BR cleanup " [\-\-keep N] [\-\-dry-run]"
Remove owned, inactive runtime releases outside the retention set.
The default retention count is 2; N must be at least 2.
Current, previous and leased releases remain protected.
Then remove cached source archives that neither the project pin nor a
retained release refers to; install and update do the same after activation.
.B \-\-dry-run
reports eligible release identifiers and archives without deleting them.
This command does not remove account data or uninstall Termux packages.
.TP
.BR self-update " [\-\-version X.Y.Z] [\-f|\-\-force] [\-V|\-\-verbose] [\-\-json]"
Download a published Termux Muscle source release, verify its checksums,
compile it locally and run its offline mandatory tests before publishing the
new management tool.
Ordinary output names each stage and prints one dot for each completed top
level test program, followed by program totals. On a terminal, a spinner
with elapsed seconds runs during the build, and a live status after the dots
shows the running test program, its position and its elapsed seconds; each
is erased when its step ends. Set TM_SELF_UPDATE_PROGRESS_LIVE=0 to turn the
live status off. A failure names its stage and leaves a private full log at
the printed path.
Without
.BR \-\-version ,
resolve the latest published project release.
If the resolved version is equal to or older than the installed manager,
print a notice without downloading the installer. An equal version exits 2
with category already_current; an older target exits 3 with category
target_older. Exit 0 means the update or forced reinstall completed; other
update failures exit 1. Signal interruption may use the shell's signal status.
.BR \-f " or " \-\-force
permits an intentional reinstall or compatible downgrade, while retaining
the normal checksum, build, test and ownership checks.
.B \-\-json
prints one versioned JSON result on standard output. The object includes the
current and target manager versions, forced flag, result, exit code, error
code and message. Its transcript contains complete combined output as base64
bytes when within the 32 MiB bound. A capture limit failure exits 1 and marks
the transcript incomplete.
.BR \-V " or " \-\-verbose
streams the full build, test and installer output as it happens. It has no
effect when combined with
.BR \-\-json ;
the JSON transcript already includes all captured output.
This command leaves the current Claude Code runtime selected.
An in-place downgrade to older management releases that cannot read the
current ownership records is rejected.
This guard applies to the new self-update command.
Do not rerun an old immutable installer over a newer installation:
older installers cannot read the new manual ownership record and can partially
downgrade the tool before failing.
Recover such an attempt by rerunning the newer verified installer.
If a management downgrade is necessary, uninstall with the current manager
before using the older installer.
Management files are versioned and leased; publication selects the new
version atomically and retains the previous tooling version.
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove owned runtime and management files and restore eligible managed entries.
Close other running managed sessions first; active leases prevent removal.
Unrelated files, externally changed entries, Claude credentials, settings,
sessions and projects are preserved.
Installed Termux dependencies, including the manual viewer, remain installed.
.PP
.RS
When safe removal needs to retain changed entries or recovery evidence,
the command reports this and leaves
.I UNINSTALL-RECOVERY.txt
inside the remaining installation root.
Inspect that evidence instead of deleting ownership records to force cleanup.
.RE
.SH VERSION SELECTION
The following options apply to
.B install
and
.BR update :
.TP
.BI \-\-claude-version " X.Y.Z|latest|stable|pinned"
Choose the Claude Code release.
.B latest
(the default) and
.B stable
read Anthropic's release channel at
.IR https://downloads.claude.ai/claude-code-releases/ ;
stable is Anthropic's delayed channel.
.B pinned
is the release this Termux Muscle version was device-tested with, and
.I X.Y.Z
names one exact release.
.PP
.RS
A release other than the pin is installed only when two things hold.
Anthropic's detached signature on that release's
.I manifest.json
must verify, with
.BR gpgv (1),
against Anthropic's release signing key, which is built into the manager
with fingerprint 31DD DE24 DDFA B679 F42D 7BD2 BAA9 29FF 1A7E CACE.
The executable extracted from the official ARM64 musl npm package must have
the SHA-256 that manifest lists for linux-arm64-musl.
Such a release is labelled
.B signed
in its receipt and in
.BR versions .
The channel file itself only names a version; it admits nothing.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " native|proot"
Choose how this release runs; see BACKENDS.
Without the option a release uses the project default, native.
.B repair
keeps the backend of the release it rebuilds.
.TP
.B \-\-allow-unverified
Install the selected release without checking Anthropic's signature.
The npm registry's archive integrity and the local candidate startup checks
still apply.
The receipt and version display keep an unverified label.
This flag does not establish device or workflow compatibility or grant
account or model access.
.TP
.B \-\-offline
Use only verified cached artifacts.
No missing metadata or archive is downloaded.
Without a version selector it rebuilds the project pin; it cannot read a
release channel.
It is also accepted by
.BR repair .
.SH BACKENDS
Anthropic's executable expects a musl loader at
.I /lib/ld-musl-aarch64.so.1
and nameservers in
.IR /etc/resolv.conf .
Android has neither path.
Each release records one of two ways of supplying them, shown by
.BR "termux-muscle versions" .
.TP
.B native
The default.
The release is prepared once, at installation, after its downloads are
verified.
.BR patchelf (1)
sets the installed executable's loader path to the release's own copy of the
loader; Anthropic's code and data are left unchanged.
Three constants in that private loader copy are replaced, so that it reads
its nameservers from descriptor 99 and reads TM_PRELOAD and TM_LIBRARY_PATH
in place of LD_PRELOAD and LD_LIBRARY_PATH.
A small object,
.IR lib/tm-resolver.so ,
opens the resolver file on descriptor 99 when a Claude Code process starts.
Claude Code and everything it starts then run as ordinary Termux processes,
untraced and at full speed, with the caller's environment intact:
termux-exec stays in LD_PRELOAD for the Android programs Claude Code runs,
so #!/usr/bin/env scripts work as in any Termux shell.
.PP
.RS
Three things differ from the PRoot backend.
Hooks run through
.IR /bin/sh ,
which is Android's shell and not Bash.
A script that Claude Code starts directly, without a shell, cannot begin
with #!/usr/bin/env or #!/bin/sh; name its interpreter as the command.
.I /tmp
is not writable; TMPDIR is.
.RE
.TP
.B proot
The downloaded executable and loader run unchanged inside PRoot, which
supplies the loader, the resolver file, Termux Bash as
.IR /bin/sh ,
.I /usr/bin/env
and a private
.IR /tmp .
PRoot traces every system call of Claude Code and of every process it
starts, which makes process creation and file access several times slower,
and a release cannot start inside another traced process.
.PP
The backend belongs to a release.
.B rollback
returns to the previous release whatever its backend, and a running session
keeps the backend it started with.
To move a version to the other backend, install it again:
.BR "termux-muscle update --claude-version X.Y.Z --backend proot" .
.SH INSTALLATION AND MANUAL
The versioned source installer obtains missing native Termux prerequisites
with the Termux package manager, builds the helper and runs offline tests.
It installs the management tool, normally installs the newest signed Claude
Code release (the installer's
.B \-\-claude-version
option chooses another), and provides this manual page for
.BR "man termux-muscle" .
The Termux package supplying
.B man
is
.BR mandoc ;
no separate groff installation is required.
.PP
The manual entry is normally
.I $PREFIX/share/man/man1/termux-muscle.1
and is an owned regular file, so the Termux manual index can find it.
Management self-update refreshes it from the new tool version only when its
contents and permissions still match the ownership record.
An unrelated pre-existing manual entry is preserved and reported.
The installed owned copy can still be read directly with
.B man
followed by its full pathname.
The manager does not change MANPATH or shell startup files.
.PP
Normal installation manages
.I $PREFIX/bin/claude
and
.IR $HOME/.local/bin/claude ,
plus the first existing executable claude on PATH when it is elsewhere.
Existing commands are backed up before takeover and the affected paths are
reported.
An existing shell alias or function may still take precedence over PATH;
shell aliases, functions and startup files are not rewritten.
.B \-\-no-link
preserves these Claude command entries.
.B \-\-link
remains an explicit synonym for the default behavior.
.PP
For a locally built checkout, the source bootstrap entry point is:
.PP
.nf
  bash bin/termux-muscle bootstrap \
    --source-dir "$PWD" --build-dir "$PWD/build"
.fi
.PP
.B \-\-no-install
installs only the management tool and manual, without downloading a runtime
or taking over Claude command entries.
Use the normal published installer for its prerequisite, source-verification,
local-build and test sequence.
.SH MIGRATION PREFLIGHT (UNRELEASED SOURCE)
Run
.B termux-muscle migration
from the affected project for sanitized JSON advisories about old settings loader
overrides, plugin/worktree path hints and executable PATH shadowing.
It reads bounded metadata only; it never rewrites configuration, scans transcripts,
executes hooks or makes network/model requests.
WARN means manual review, not necessarily a broken installation; advisories exit 0.
Parent-shell aliases/functions and caches cannot be inspected by a child process.
Use type -a claude in the same interactive shell.
.SH DIAGNOSTIC REPORTS
A default report contains the project and Claude Code versions, musl version,
manufacturer/model, Android release and API, ABI, kernel, memory page size,
Termux version/source, relevant package versions and check outcomes.
It omits authentication secrets, complete settings/environment dumps, serial
numbers, network addresses, private prompts and raw session output.
.PP
Each check is
.BR PASS ,
.BR FAIL " or " SKIP .
Checks not actually performed remain SKIP.
Version 0.3.0 also checks isolated non-conversational initialization before
activation. Fresh HOME/config/cwd and disabled customizations isolate user settings;
system managed-policy presence blocks automatic probes rather than running hooks.
Each startup probe is bounded to 30 seconds and 64 KiB captured output.
Only fixed result codes are retained.
.PP
Local version/help/initialization/namespace checks do not automatically mark installation,
update, rollback, uninstall or authenticated Claude tool workflows PASS.
DNS configuration checks inspect the selected resolver file; they make no
DNS query and do not prove external network reachability.
.PP
With
.BI \-\-output " FILE",
the parent directory must already exist and FILE must be a new path.
Existing files and symlinks are not overwritten.
Publication is atomic and uses private mode 0600.
A valid report may still be written when checks fail; the command returns
failure.
Without the option, JSON goes to standard output.
.PP
Reports are never uploaded automatically.
Review a saved report, then attach it voluntarily to an issue in the project
repository with the exact failing command and observed behavior.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Used to locate the default installation root and the ordinary Claude account
data.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
When set, selects the base of the default managed data directory.
An explicit
.B \-\-root
overrides it.
.TP
.B PREFIX
The Termux package prefix; overridden by
.BR \-\-prefix .
.TP
.B TM_RESOLV_CONF
Select a readable resolver configuration file instead of
.IR $PREFIX/etc/resolv.conf .
The PRoot backend maps the selected live file into its namespace.
The native backend passes its resolved path on in the same variable, and
each Claude Code process opens that file when it starts.
Valid resolver symlinks are supported.
The manager does not replace it with a public DNS service.
.TP
.B SSL_CERT_FILE
Select a readable TLS certificate bundle instead of
.IR $PREFIX/etc/tls/cert.pem .
.TP
.B PATH
Used for ordinary command lookup.
The installer does not edit shell startup files to change PATH.
It reports the owned management command path if a convenience entry is
unavailable.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Selects temporary storage for management operations where applicable.
On the native backend Claude Code and its tools keep the caller's TMPDIR, or
.I $PREFIX/tmp
when it is unset, and CLAUDE_CODE_TMPDIR defaults to it.
On the PRoot backend temporary files use that release's private runtime
directory.
.TP
.B LD_PRELOAD, LD_LIBRARY_PATH
The native backend leaves both as the caller set them; its private loader
does not read them.
When LD_PRELOAD is unset it supplies Termux's termux-exec library, as a
Termux shell would.
The PRoot backend clears both for the whole process tree.
.PP
The launcher disables the unmanaged updater and selects native Termux
ripgrep.
TM_PRELOAD and TM_LIBRARY_PATH belong to the native backend's private loader
and are set by the launcher.
Ordinary Claude environment and account settings otherwise remain under the
user's control.
Internal TM_* lease/context variables are not a public configuration interface.
.SH FILES
All paths below ROOT are managed implementation state, not files to edit
manually.
.TP
.I ROOT/installation.json
Installation identity.
.TP
.I ROOT/state.json
Current/previous runtime selection and lifecycle state.
.TP
.I ROOT/releases/RELEASE_ID/
Owned executable, loader, receipt, runtime context and private temporary files;
for a native release also
.IR lib/tm-resolver.so .
.TP
.I ROOT/releases/RELEASE_ID/payload.json
Recorded source identities, file hashes, version, backend and compatibility
provenance.
A native release also records the hashes of its prepared files.
.TP
.I ROOT/cache/
Content-addressed source archives, reverified before reuse.
Only the archives of the project pin and of retained releases are kept.
.TP
.I ROOT/tools/current
Atomic symlink selecting the active management-tool directory.
.TP
.I ROOT/tools/TOOL_ID/docs/man/termux-muscle.1
This manual as shipped with that tool version.
.TP
.I ROOT/tooling.json
Tooling publication state and owned stable-entry information.
.TP
.I ROOT/links.json
Ownership and restoration records for managed external entries.
.TP
.I ROOT/backups/
Original files and retained recovery evidence.
.TP
.I ROOT/bin/termux-muscle
Owned management launcher.
.TP
.I ROOT/bin/claude
Owned convenience launcher for the current runtime.
.TP
.I ROOT/UNINSTALL-RECOVERY.txt
Explanation left when removal preserves conflict or recovery information.
.PP
A remaining lock file alone does not prove that maintenance is running.
The operating system releases the process-held mutation lock after process exit.
Do not delete ownership metadata to bypass an error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Management commands return 0 on success and nonzero on an error.
The text diagnostic includes a category describing the failing boundary.
.B doctor
and
.B test
return nonzero if any reported check/model result is FAIL.
A SKIP alone is not a failure and is not evidence of success.
.B run
forwards execution to the upstream runtime; its exit behavior follows that
process.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect the manager and runtime versions:
.PP
.nf
  termux-muscle --version
  termux-muscle versions
  termux-muscle run -- --version
.fi
.PP
Start Claude or authenticate through its own flow:
.PP
.nf
  claude
  claude auth login
.fi
.PP
Check health and save a report without model usage:
.PP
.nf
  termux-muscle doctor
  termux-muscle test --output report.json
.fi
.PP
Explicitly probe a model available to the account:
.PP
.nf
  termux-muscle test --model MODEL_ID --output model-report.json
.fi
.PP
Move to the newest Claude Code release and recover locally:
.PP
.nf
  termux-muscle update
  termux-muscle rollback
  termux-muscle repair --offline
  termux-muscle cleanup --dry-run
.fi
.PP
See which Claude Code versions can be installed:
.PP
.nf
  termux-muscle versions --available
  termux-muscle versions --available --all
.fi
.PP
Choose a particular Claude Code release:
.PP
.nf
  termux-muscle update --claude-version stable
  termux-muscle update --claude-version 2.1.295
  termux-muscle update --claude-version pinned
.fi
.PP
Upgrade the management tool separately:
.PP
.nf
  termux-muscle self-update
.fi
.PP
Select an independent installation without changing account settings:
.PP
.nf
  termux-muscle --root "$HOME/test-runtime" install
  termux-muscle --root "$HOME/test-runtime" run -- --help
.fi
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
If a command named claude reports a missing native binary, compare
.B command -v claude
with
.BR "termux-muscle run -- --version" .
A different npm wrapper may still own that command.
Use the manager directly and inspect link ownership before requesting
replacement.
.PP
An integrity failure is different from a DNS/TLS failure or rejected login.
Start with
.B doctor
and the exact error category.
Account authentication should use the upstream login flow; reinstalling a
runtime does not refresh an OAuth token or grant access to a model.
.PP
Run maintenance of a PRoot release from a fresh native Termux shell when an
existing PRoot session is pinned to another release or installation.
If something works under PRoot and not natively, see BACKENDS for the three
differences, and reinstall the version with
.B \-\-backend proot
while reporting it.
Android background-process limits, vendor restrictions, cross-session messaging,
MCP transports, hooks and authenticated tool workflows require separate tests.
